---
id: virtual-file-routes
title: Virtual File Routes
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We'd like to thank the Remix team for "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjTX8hQTlEc&t=730s","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pioneering the concept of virtual file routes"}]},{"type":"text","value":". We've taken inspiration from their work and adapted it to work with TanStack Router's existing file-based route-tree generation."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual file routes are a powerful concept that allows you to build a route tree programmatically using code that references real files in your project. This can be useful if:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have an existing route organization that you want to keep."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to customize the location of your route files."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to completely override TanStack Router's file-based route generation and build your own convention."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's a quick example of using virtual file routes to map a route tree to a set of real files in your project:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport {\n  rootRoute,\n  route,\n  index,\n  layout,\n  physical,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual file routes can be configured either via:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackRouter"},{"type":"text","value":" plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsr.config.json"},{"type":"text","value":" file for the TanStack Router CLI"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-via-the-tanstackrouter-plugin","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration via the TanStackRouter Plugin"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you're using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackRouter"},{"type":"text","value":" plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack, you can configure virtual file routes by passing the path of your routes file to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"virtualRoutesConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" option when setting up the plugin:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"framework","attributes":{},"children":[{"type":"component","name":"framework-panel","tagName":"md-framework-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-framework":"react"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'react',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})","lang":"tsx","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]},{"type":"component","name":"framework-panel","tagName":"md-framework-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-framework":"solid"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'solid',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})","lang":"tsx","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]}],"properties":{"data-available-frameworks":"[\"react\",\"solid\"]","data-framework-meta":"{\"codeBlocksByFramework\":{\"react\":[{\"title\":\"vite.config.ts\",\"code\":\"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [\\n    tanstackRouter({\\n      target: 'react',\\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\\n    }),\\n    react(),\\n  ],\\n})\",\"language\":\"tsx\"}],\"solid\":[{\"title\":\"vite.config.ts\",\"code\":\"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [\\n    tanstackRouter({\\n      target: 'solid',\\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\\n    }),\\n    solid(),\\n  ],\\n})\",\"language\":\"tsx\"}]}}"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or, you choose to define the virtual routes directly in the configuration:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes }), react()],\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"component","name":"framework","attributes":{},"children":[{"type":"component","name":"framework-panel","tagName":"md-framework-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-framework":"react"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'react' }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})","lang":"tsx","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]},{"type":"component","name":"framework-panel","tagName":"md-framework-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-framework":"solid"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'solid' }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})","lang":"tsx","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]}],"properties":{"data-available-frameworks":"[\"react\",\"solid\"]","data-framework-meta":"{\"codeBlocksByFramework\":{\"react\":[{\"title\":\"vite.config.ts\",\"code\":\"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\\n\\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\\n])\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [\\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'react' }),\\n    react(),\\n  ],\\n})\",\"language\":\"tsx\"}],\"solid\":[{\"title\":\"vite.config.ts\",\"code\":\"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\\n\\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\\n])\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [\\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'solid' }),\\n    solid(),\\n  ],\\n})\",\"language\":\"tsx\"}]}}"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-virtual-file-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating Virtual File Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To create virtual file routes, you'll need to import the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/virtual-file-routes"},{"type":"text","value":" package. This package provides a set of functions that allow you to create virtual routes that reference real files in your project. A few utility functions are exported from the package:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rootRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a virtual root route."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a virtual route."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a virtual index route."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a virtual pathless layout route."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"physical"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a physical virtual route (more on this later)."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"virtual-root-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual Root Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rootRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" function is used to create a virtual root route. It takes a file name and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual root route:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... children routes\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"virtual-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route"},{"type":"text","value":" function is used to create a virtual route. It takes a path, a file name, and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual route:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route"},{"type":"text","value":" receives an explicit URL path, leading and trailing underscores are treated literally. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":" to create pathless layout routes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also define a virtual route without a file name. This allows to set a common path prefix for its children:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/hello', [\n    route('/world', 'world.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/world\"\n    route('/universe', 'universe.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/universe\"\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"virtual-index-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual Index Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"},{"type":"text","value":" function is used to create a virtual index route. It takes a file name. Here's an example of a virtual index route:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { index } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [index('index.tsx')])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"virtual-pathless-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual Pathless Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":" function is used to create a virtual pathless route. It takes a file name, an array of children routes, and an optional pathless ID. Here's an example of a virtual pathless route:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also specify a pathless ID to give the route a unique identifier that is different from the filename:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('my-pathless-layout-id', 'pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"physical-virtual-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Physical Virtual Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Physical virtual routes are a way to \"mount\" a directory of good ol' TanStack Router File Based routing convention under a specific URL path. This can be useful if you are using virtual routes to customize a small portion of your route tree high up in the hierarchy, but want to use the standard file-based routing convention for sub-routes and directories."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider the following file structure:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"/routes\n├── root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── pathlessLayout.tsx\n├── app\n│   ├── dashboard.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-index.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-invoices.tsx\n│   ├── invoices-index.tsx\n│   ├── invoice-detail.tsx\n└── posts\n    ├── index.tsx\n    ├── $postId.tsx\n    ├── $postId.edit.tsx\n    ├── comments/\n    │   ├── index.tsx\n    │   ├── $commentId.tsx\n    └── likes/\n        ├── index.tsx\n        ├── $likeId.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let's use virtual routes to customize our route tree for everything but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts"},{"type":"text","value":", then use physical virtual routes to mount the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts"},{"type":"text","value":" directory under the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts"},{"type":"text","value":" path:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // Set up your virtual routes as normal\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    // Mount the `posts` directory under the `/posts` path\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"merging-physical-routes-at-current-level","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Merging Physical Routes at Current Level"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"physical"},{"type":"text","value":" with an empty path prefix (or a single argument) to merge routes from a physical directory directly at the current level, without adding a path prefix. This is useful when you want to organize your routes into separate directories but have them appear at the same URL level."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider the following file structure:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n└── features\n    ├── index.tsx\n    └── contact.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can merge the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" directory routes at the root level:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes.ts\nimport { physical, rootRoute, route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('__root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx'),\n  // Merge features/ routes at root level (no path prefix)\n  physical('features'),\n  // Or equivalently: physical('', 'features')\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This will produce the following routes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/about"},{"type":"text","value":" - from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"about.tsx"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/"},{"type":"text","value":" - from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features/index.tsx"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/contact"},{"type":"text","value":" - from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features/contact.tsx"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" When merging at the same level, ensure there are no conflicting route paths between your virtual routes and the physical directory routes. If a conflict occurs (e.g., both have an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/about"},{"type":"text","value":" route), the generator will throw an error."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"virtual-routes-inside-of-tanstack-router-file-based-routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual Routes inside of TanStack Router File Based routing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The previous section showed you how you can use TanStack Router's File Based routing convention inside of a virtual route configuration.\nHowever, the opposite is possible as well.\nYou can configure the main part of your app's route tree using TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and opt into virtual route configuration for specific subtrees."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider the following file structure:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── foo\n│   ├── bar\n│   │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   │   ├── details.tsx\n│   │   ├── home.tsx\n│   │   └── route.ts\n│   └── bar.tsx\n└── index.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let's look at the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bar"},{"type":"text","value":" directory which contains a special file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__virtual.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". This file instructs the generator to switch over to virtual file route configuration for this directory (and its child directories)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__virtual.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" configures the virtual routes for that particular subtree of the route tree. It uses the same API as explained above, with the only difference being that no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rootRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" is defined for that subtree:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/foo/bar/__virtual.ts\nimport {\n  defineVirtualSubtreeConfig,\n  index,\n  route,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport default defineVirtualSubtreeConfig([\n  index('home.tsx'),\n  route('$id', 'details.tsx'),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The helper function "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineVirtualSubtreeConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" is closely modeled after vite's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" and allows you to define a subtree configuration via a default export. The default export can either be"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a subtree config object"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a function returning a subtree config object"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an async function returning a subtree config object"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"inception","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inception"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can mix and match TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and virtual route configuration however you like.\nLet's go deeper!\nCheck out the following example that starts off using File Based routing convention, switches over to virtual route configuration for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts"},{"type":"text","value":", switches back to File Based routing convention for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/lets-go"},{"type":"text","value":" only to switch over to virtual route configuration again for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/lets-go/deeper"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── posts\n│   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   ├── details.tsx\n│   ├── home.tsx\n│   └── lets-go\n│       ├── deeper\n│       │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│       │   └── home.tsx\n│       └── index.tsx\n└── posts.tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-via-the-tanstack-router-cli","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration via the TanStack Router CLI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you're using the TanStack Router CLI, you can configure virtual file routes by defining the path to your routes file in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsr.config.json"},{"type":"text","value":" file:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": \"./routes.ts\"\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or you can define the virtual routes directly in the configuration, while much less common allows you to configure them via the TanStack Router CLI by adding a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"virtualRouteConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" object to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsr.config.json"},{"type":"text","value":" file and defining your virtual routes and passing the resulting JSON that is generated by calling the actual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rootRoute"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"},{"type":"text","value":"/etc functions from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/virtual-file-routes"},{"type":"text","value":" package:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": {\n    \"type\": \"root\",\n    \"file\": \"root.tsx\",\n    \"children\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"index\",\n        \"file\": \"home.tsx\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"route\",\n        \"file\": \"posts/posts.tsx\",\n        \"path\": \"/posts\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"index\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-home.tsx\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"route\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-detail.tsx\",\n            \"path\": \"$postId\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"layout\",\n        \"id\": \"first\",\n        \"file\": \"layout/first-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"layout\",\n            \"id\": \"second\",\n            \"file\": \"layout/second-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n            \"children\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"a.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-a\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"b.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-b\"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}","lang":"json"}],"headings":[{"id":"configuration","text":"Configuration","level":2},{"id":"configuration-via-the-tanstackrouter-plugin","text":"Configuration via the TanStackRouter Plugin","level":2},{"id":"creating-virtual-file-routes","text":"Creating Virtual File Routes","level":2},{"id":"virtual-root-route","text":"Virtual Root Route","level":2},{"id":"virtual-route","text":"Virtual Route","level":2},{"id":"virtual-index-route","text":"Virtual Index Route","level":2},{"id":"virtual-pathless-route","text":"Virtual Pathless Route","level":2},{"id":"physical-virtual-routes","text":"Physical Virtual Routes","level":2},{"id":"merging-physical-routes-at-current-level","text":"Merging Physical Routes at Current Level","level":3},{"id":"virtual-routes-inside-of-tanstack-router-file-based-routing","text":"Virtual Routes inside of TanStack Router File Based routing","level":2},{"id":"inception","text":"Inception","level":2},{"id":"configuration-via-the-tanstack-router-cli","text":"Configuration via the TanStack Router CLI","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","solid"],"downloadMarkdown":"\n> We'd like to thank the Remix team for [pioneering the concept of virtual file routes](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjTX8hQTlEc&t=730s). We've taken inspiration from their work and adapted it to work with TanStack Router's existing file-based route-tree generation.\n\nVirtual file routes are a powerful concept that allows you to build a route tree programmatically using code that references real files in your project. This can be useful if:\n\n- You have an existing route organization that you want to keep.\n- You want to customize the location of your route files.\n- You want to completely override TanStack Router's file-based route generation and build your own convention.\n\nHere's a quick example of using virtual file routes to map a route tree to a set of real files in your project:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport {\n  rootRoute,\n  route,\n  index,\n  layout,\n  physical,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nVirtual file routes can be configured either via:\n\n- The `TanStackRouter` plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack\n- The `tsr.config.json` file for the TanStack Router CLI\n\n## Configuration via the TanStackRouter Plugin\n\nIf you're using the `TanStackRouter` plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack, you can configure virtual file routes by passing the path of your routes file to the `virtualRoutesConfig` option when setting up the plugin:\n\n<!-- ::start:framework -->\n\n# React\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'react',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Solid\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'solid',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:framework -->\n\nOr, you choose to define the virtual routes directly in the configuration:\n\n```tsx\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes }), react()],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::start:framework -->\n\n# React\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'react' }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Solid\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'solid' }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:framework -->\n\n## Creating Virtual File Routes\n\nTo create virtual file routes, you'll need to import the `@tanstack/virtual-file-routes` package. This package provides a set of functions that allow you to create virtual routes that reference real files in your project. A few utility functions are exported from the package:\n\n- `rootRoute` - Creates a virtual root route.\n- `route` - Creates a virtual route.\n- `index` - Creates a virtual index route.\n- `layout` - Creates a virtual pathless layout route.\n- `physical` - Creates a physical virtual route (more on this later).\n\n## Virtual Root Route\n\nThe `rootRoute` function is used to create a virtual root route. It takes a file name and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual root route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... children routes\n])\n```\n\n## Virtual Route\n\nThe `route` function is used to create a virtual route. It takes a path, a file name, and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\nBecause `route` receives an explicit URL path, leading and trailing underscores are treated literally. Use `layout` to create pathless layout routes.\n\nYou can also define a virtual route without a file name. This allows to set a common path prefix for its children:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/hello', [\n    route('/world', 'world.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/world\"\n    route('/universe', 'universe.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/universe\"\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Virtual Index Route\n\nThe `index` function is used to create a virtual index route. It takes a file name. Here's an example of a virtual index route:\n\n```tsx\nimport { index } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [index('index.tsx')])\n```\n\n## Virtual Pathless Route\n\nThe `layout` function is used to create a virtual pathless route. It takes a file name, an array of children routes, and an optional pathless ID. Here's an example of a virtual pathless route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\nYou can also specify a pathless ID to give the route a unique identifier that is different from the filename:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('my-pathless-layout-id', 'pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Physical Virtual Routes\n\nPhysical virtual routes are a way to \"mount\" a directory of good ol' TanStack Router File Based routing convention under a specific URL path. This can be useful if you are using virtual routes to customize a small portion of your route tree high up in the hierarchy, but want to use the standard file-based routing convention for sub-routes and directories.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── pathlessLayout.tsx\n├── app\n│   ├── dashboard.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-index.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-invoices.tsx\n│   ├── invoices-index.tsx\n│   ├── invoice-detail.tsx\n└── posts\n    ├── index.tsx\n    ├── $postId.tsx\n    ├── $postId.edit.tsx\n    ├── comments/\n    │   ├── index.tsx\n    │   ├── $commentId.tsx\n    └── likes/\n        ├── index.tsx\n        ├── $likeId.tsx\n```\n\nLet's use virtual routes to customize our route tree for everything but `posts`, then use physical virtual routes to mount the `posts` directory under the `/posts` path:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // Set up your virtual routes as normal\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    // Mount the `posts` directory under the `/posts` path\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n### Merging Physical Routes at Current Level\n\nYou can also use `physical` with an empty path prefix (or a single argument) to merge routes from a physical directory directly at the current level, without adding a path prefix. This is useful when you want to organize your routes into separate directories but have them appear at the same URL level.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n└── features\n    ├── index.tsx\n    └── contact.tsx\n```\n\nYou can merge the `features` directory routes at the root level:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { physical, rootRoute, route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('__root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx'),\n  // Merge features/ routes at root level (no path prefix)\n  physical('features'),\n  // Or equivalently: physical('', 'features')\n])\n```\n\nThis will produce the following routes:\n\n- `/about` - from `about.tsx`\n- `/` - from `features/index.tsx`\n- `/contact` - from `features/contact.tsx`\n\n> **Note:** When merging at the same level, ensure there are no conflicting route paths between your virtual routes and the physical directory routes. If a conflict occurs (e.g., both have an `/about` route), the generator will throw an error.\n\n## Virtual Routes inside of TanStack Router File Based routing\n\nThe previous section showed you how you can use TanStack Router's File Based routing convention inside of a virtual route configuration.\nHowever, the opposite is possible as well.  \nYou can configure the main part of your app's route tree using TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and opt into virtual route configuration for specific subtrees.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── foo\n│   ├── bar\n│   │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   │   ├── details.tsx\n│   │   ├── home.tsx\n│   │   └── route.ts\n│   └── bar.tsx\n└── index.tsx\n```\n\nLet's look at the `bar` directory which contains a special file named `__virtual.ts`. This file instructs the generator to switch over to virtual file route configuration for this directory (and its child directories).\n\n`__virtual.ts` configures the virtual routes for that particular subtree of the route tree. It uses the same API as explained above, with the only difference being that no `rootRoute` is defined for that subtree:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/foo/bar/__virtual.ts\nimport {\n  defineVirtualSubtreeConfig,\n  index,\n  route,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport default defineVirtualSubtreeConfig([\n  index('home.tsx'),\n  route('$id', 'details.tsx'),\n])\n```\n\nThe helper function `defineVirtualSubtreeConfig` is closely modeled after vite's `defineConfig` and allows you to define a subtree configuration via a default export. The default export can either be\n\n- a subtree config object\n- a function returning a subtree config object\n- an async function returning a subtree config object\n\n## Inception\n\nYou can mix and match TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and virtual route configuration however you like.  \nLet's go deeper!  \nCheck out the following example that starts off using File Based routing convention, switches over to virtual route configuration for `/posts`, switches back to File Based routing convention for `/posts/lets-go` only to switch over to virtual route configuration again for `/posts/lets-go/deeper`.\n\n```\n├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── posts\n│   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   ├── details.tsx\n│   ├── home.tsx\n│   └── lets-go\n│       ├── deeper\n│       │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│       │   └── home.tsx\n│       └── index.tsx\n└── posts.tsx\n```\n\n## Configuration via the TanStack Router CLI\n\nIf you're using the TanStack Router CLI, you can configure virtual file routes by defining the path to your routes file in the `tsr.config.json` file:\n\n```json\n// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": \"./routes.ts\"\n}\n```\n\nOr you can define the virtual routes directly in the configuration, while much less common allows you to configure them via the TanStack Router CLI by adding a `virtualRouteConfig` object to your `tsr.config.json` file and defining your virtual routes and passing the resulting JSON that is generated by calling the actual `rootRoute`/`route`/`index`/etc functions from the `@tanstack/virtual-file-routes` package:\n\n```json\n// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": {\n    \"type\": \"root\",\n    \"file\": \"root.tsx\",\n    \"children\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"index\",\n        \"file\": \"home.tsx\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"route\",\n        \"file\": \"posts/posts.tsx\",\n        \"path\": \"/posts\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"index\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-home.tsx\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"route\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-detail.tsx\",\n            \"path\": \"$postId\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"layout\",\n        \"id\": \"first\",\n        \"file\": \"layout/first-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"layout\",\n            \"id\": \"second\",\n            \"file\": \"layout/second-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n            \"children\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"a.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-a\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"b.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-b\"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: virtual-file-routes\ntitle: Virtual File Routes\n---\n\n> We'd like to thank the Remix team for [pioneering the concept of virtual file routes](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjTX8hQTlEc&t=730s). We've taken inspiration from their work and adapted it to work with TanStack Router's existing file-based route-tree generation.\n\nVirtual file routes are a powerful concept that allows you to build a route tree programmatically using code that references real files in your project. This can be useful if:\n\n- You have an existing route organization that you want to keep.\n- You want to customize the location of your route files.\n- You want to completely override TanStack Router's file-based route generation and build your own convention.\n\nHere's a quick example of using virtual file routes to map a route tree to a set of real files in your project:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport {\n  rootRoute,\n  route,\n  index,\n  layout,\n  physical,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nVirtual file routes can be configured either via:\n\n- The `TanStackRouter` plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack\n- The `tsr.config.json` file for the TanStack Router CLI\n\n## Configuration via the TanStackRouter Plugin\n\nIf you're using the `TanStackRouter` plugin for Vite/Rspack/Webpack, you can configure virtual file routes by passing the path of your routes file to the `virtualRoutesConfig` option when setting up the plugin:\n\n<!-- ::start:framework -->\n\n# React\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'react',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Solid\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({\n      target: 'solid',\n      virtualRouteConfig: './routes.ts',\n    }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:framework -->\n\nOr, you choose to define the virtual routes directly in the configuration:\n\n```tsx\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes }), react()],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::start:framework -->\n\n# React\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'react' }),\n    react(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Solid\n\n```tsx title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport solid from 'vite-plugin-solid'\nimport { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... the rest of your virtual route tree\n])\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackRouter({ virtualRouteConfig: routes, target: 'solid' }),\n    solid(),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:framework -->\n\n## Creating Virtual File Routes\n\nTo create virtual file routes, you'll need to import the `@tanstack/virtual-file-routes` package. This package provides a set of functions that allow you to create virtual routes that reference real files in your project. A few utility functions are exported from the package:\n\n- `rootRoute` - Creates a virtual root route.\n- `route` - Creates a virtual route.\n- `index` - Creates a virtual index route.\n- `layout` - Creates a virtual pathless layout route.\n- `physical` - Creates a physical virtual route (more on this later).\n\n## Virtual Root Route\n\nThe `rootRoute` function is used to create a virtual root route. It takes a file name and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual root route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { rootRoute } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // ... children routes\n])\n```\n\n## Virtual Route\n\nThe `route` function is used to create a virtual route. It takes a path, a file name, and an array of children routes. Here's an example of a virtual route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\nBecause `route` receives an explicit URL path, leading and trailing underscores are treated literally. Use `layout` to create pathless layout routes.\n\nYou can also define a virtual route without a file name. This allows to set a common path prefix for its children:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  route('/hello', [\n    route('/world', 'world.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/world\"\n    route('/universe', 'universe.tsx'), // full path will be \"/hello/universe\"\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Virtual Index Route\n\nThe `index` function is used to create a virtual index route. It takes a file name. Here's an example of a virtual index route:\n\n```tsx\nimport { index } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nconst routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [index('index.tsx')])\n```\n\n## Virtual Pathless Route\n\nThe `layout` function is used to create a virtual pathless route. It takes a file name, an array of children routes, and an optional pathless ID. Here's an example of a virtual pathless route:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\nYou can also specify a pathless ID to give the route a unique identifier that is different from the filename:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { layout } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  layout('my-pathless-layout-id', 'pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    // ... children routes\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n## Physical Virtual Routes\n\nPhysical virtual routes are a way to \"mount\" a directory of good ol' TanStack Router File Based routing convention under a specific URL path. This can be useful if you are using virtual routes to customize a small portion of your route tree high up in the hierarchy, but want to use the standard file-based routing convention for sub-routes and directories.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── pathlessLayout.tsx\n├── app\n│   ├── dashboard.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-index.tsx\n│   ├── dashboard-invoices.tsx\n│   ├── invoices-index.tsx\n│   ├── invoice-detail.tsx\n└── posts\n    ├── index.tsx\n    ├── $postId.tsx\n    ├── $postId.edit.tsx\n    ├── comments/\n    │   ├── index.tsx\n    │   ├── $commentId.tsx\n    └── likes/\n        ├── index.tsx\n        ├── $likeId.tsx\n```\n\nLet's use virtual routes to customize our route tree for everything but `posts`, then use physical virtual routes to mount the `posts` directory under the `/posts` path:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nexport const routes = rootRoute('root.tsx', [\n  // Set up your virtual routes as normal\n  index('index.tsx'),\n  layout('pathlessLayout.tsx', [\n    route('/dashboard', 'app/dashboard.tsx', [\n      index('app/dashboard-index.tsx'),\n      route('/invoices', 'app/dashboard-invoices.tsx', [\n        index('app/invoices-index.tsx'),\n        route('$id', 'app/invoice-detail.tsx'),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    // Mount the `posts` directory under the `/posts` path\n    physical('/posts', 'posts'),\n  ]),\n])\n```\n\n### Merging Physical Routes at Current Level\n\nYou can also use `physical` with an empty path prefix (or a single argument) to merge routes from a physical directory directly at the current level, without adding a path prefix. This is useful when you want to organize your routes into separate directories but have them appear at the same URL level.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n└── features\n    ├── index.tsx\n    └── contact.tsx\n```\n\nYou can merge the `features` directory routes at the root level:\n\n```tsx\n// routes.ts\nimport { physical, rootRoute, route } from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport const routes = rootRoute('__root.tsx', [\n  route('/about', 'about.tsx'),\n  // Merge features/ routes at root level (no path prefix)\n  physical('features'),\n  // Or equivalently: physical('', 'features')\n])\n```\n\nThis will produce the following routes:\n\n- `/about` - from `about.tsx`\n- `/` - from `features/index.tsx`\n- `/contact` - from `features/contact.tsx`\n\n> **Note:** When merging at the same level, ensure there are no conflicting route paths between your virtual routes and the physical directory routes. If a conflict occurs (e.g., both have an `/about` route), the generator will throw an error.\n\n## Virtual Routes inside of TanStack Router File Based routing\n\nThe previous section showed you how you can use TanStack Router's File Based routing convention inside of a virtual route configuration.\nHowever, the opposite is possible as well.  \nYou can configure the main part of your app's route tree using TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and opt into virtual route configuration for specific subtrees.\n\nConsider the following file structure:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── foo\n│   ├── bar\n│   │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   │   ├── details.tsx\n│   │   ├── home.tsx\n│   │   └── route.ts\n│   └── bar.tsx\n└── index.tsx\n```\n\nLet's look at the `bar` directory which contains a special file named `__virtual.ts`. This file instructs the generator to switch over to virtual file route configuration for this directory (and its child directories).\n\n`__virtual.ts` configures the virtual routes for that particular subtree of the route tree. It uses the same API as explained above, with the only difference being that no `rootRoute` is defined for that subtree:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/foo/bar/__virtual.ts\nimport {\n  defineVirtualSubtreeConfig,\n  index,\n  route,\n} from '@tanstack/virtual-file-routes'\n\nexport default defineVirtualSubtreeConfig([\n  index('home.tsx'),\n  route('$id', 'details.tsx'),\n])\n```\n\nThe helper function `defineVirtualSubtreeConfig` is closely modeled after vite's `defineConfig` and allows you to define a subtree configuration via a default export. The default export can either be\n\n- a subtree config object\n- a function returning a subtree config object\n- an async function returning a subtree config object\n\n## Inception\n\nYou can mix and match TanStack Router's File Based routing convention and virtual route configuration however you like.  \nLet's go deeper!  \nCheck out the following example that starts off using File Based routing convention, switches over to virtual route configuration for `/posts`, switches back to File Based routing convention for `/posts/lets-go` only to switch over to virtual route configuration again for `/posts/lets-go/deeper`.\n\n```\n├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── posts\n│   ├── __virtual.ts\n│   ├── details.tsx\n│   ├── home.tsx\n│   └── lets-go\n│       ├── deeper\n│       │   ├── __virtual.ts\n│       │   └── home.tsx\n│       └── index.tsx\n└── posts.tsx\n```\n\n## Configuration via the TanStack Router CLI\n\nIf you're using the TanStack Router CLI, you can configure virtual file routes by defining the path to your routes file in the `tsr.config.json` file:\n\n```json\n// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": \"./routes.ts\"\n}\n```\n\nOr you can define the virtual routes directly in the configuration, while much less common allows you to configure them via the TanStack Router CLI by adding a `virtualRouteConfig` object to your `tsr.config.json` file and defining your virtual routes and passing the resulting JSON that is generated by calling the actual `rootRoute`/`route`/`index`/etc functions from the `@tanstack/virtual-file-routes` package:\n\n```json\n// tsr.config.json\n{\n  \"virtualRouteConfig\": {\n    \"type\": \"root\",\n    \"file\": \"root.tsx\",\n    \"children\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"index\",\n        \"file\": \"home.tsx\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"route\",\n        \"file\": \"posts/posts.tsx\",\n        \"path\": \"/posts\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"index\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-home.tsx\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"route\",\n            \"file\": \"posts/posts-detail.tsx\",\n            \"path\": \"$postId\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"layout\",\n        \"id\": \"first\",\n        \"file\": \"layout/first-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n        \"children\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"layout\",\n            \"id\": \"second\",\n            \"file\": \"layout/second-pathless-layout.tsx\",\n            \"children\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"a.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-a\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"route\",\n                \"file\": \"b.tsx\",\n                \"path\": \"/route-b\"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n"}
